# Rank 1251 / CVE-2024-28233 (GHSA-7r3h-4ph8-w38g): JupyterHub's
# HubOAuth cookie-authentication path (jupyterhub/services/auth.py, used by
# both the single-user server mixin and Hub-managed services) accepted any
# request carrying a valid secure session cookie with no XSRF or Origin
# check at all -- HubOAuth._get_token_cookie() calls
# handler.get_secure_cookie(self.cookie_name) and HubOAuth._get_user_cookie()
# hands the decoded token straight to user_for_token(), so a same-site
# subdomain that tosses a cookie onto the Hub's path (or, in a
# single-origin deployment, a user's own notebook content running under the
# same origin) can drive state-changing Hub/API/service requests carrying
# the ambient cookie (CWE-352/CWE-346). Pool commit
# e2798a088f5ad45340fe79cdf1386198e664f77f is the real fix merge
# (GHSA-7r3h-4ph8-w38g, COMMIT_OK): 3fa60e6849668ec1be35082d7f6e81f3db96222d
# adds jupyterhub/_xsrf_utils.py and threads check_xsrf_cookie() through
# HubOAuth._get_user_cookie() (a new _needs_check_xsrf() gate on
# Sec-Fetch-Mode/method) and through BaseHandler.prepare(), alongside
# unrelated hardening (the __Host- cookie prefix, tightened
# frame-ancestors, cookie_host_prefix_enabled, allow_token_in_url).
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap. The concept this needs already ships:
# code.CookieAuthHandlerMissingPrepareOriginCheck / VYQL-RF-044 (rank 1219,
# CVE-2015-5607, vyql/bindings/python/cookie_auth_origin_check.vyql) is a
# general Python binding, gated on dependency("tornado"), that fires at
# node.scope == "module" whenever a module calls get_secure_cookie and
# defines neither an Origin/Host netloc comparison (urlparse(...).netloc)
# nor Tornado's own check_xsrf_cookie/xsrf_cookies alternative anywhere in
# the file -- exactly what jupyterhub/services/auth.py's vulnerable
# revision is: `grep -ni "xsrf\|urlparse"` on the real file at the fix's
# parent commit (e2798a088f5ad45340fe79cdf1386198e664f77f^) returns nothing,
# and the fixed revision adds both. Confirmed the binding logic is right by
# copying the real, unmodified HubOAuth._get_token_cookie/_get_user_cookie
# block (lines 750-767 of the vulnerable services/auth.py) to just after the
# file's imports: VYQL-RF-044 fires there. Left at its real position, after
# ~700 lines of HubAuth's own Traitlets Unicode/Dict/Bool config option
# definitions (each with a multi-line help docstring), the identical,
# byte-for-byte block is invisible -- confirmed with `vyql scan --cache off`
# on a minimal repro (services/auth.py plus a requirements.txt declaring
# tornado): 9 findings, none of them VYQL-RF-044.
#
# The cause is the same shape already recorded for rank 1223's Java
# class-scope gap, but for Python's module scope: pyModuleContext
# (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/python.go:81) builds
# c.moduleTokens by walking the whole module with
# pyStructuredContextTokensScoped, whose `add` closure
# (python.go:797) and node walk (python.go:814) both hard-stop, mid-walk,
# the instant len(out) reaches pyContextBaseTokenLimit (512,
# python_context_control.go:10) -- not a per-output cap, a walk that never
# reaches the rest of the tree. A module with enough distinct
# identifiers/literals/call-paths ahead of the vulnerable call exhausts the
# budget before the walk ever gets there, and every node.scope == "module"
# Python presence binding, VYQL-RF-044 included, goes blind to everything
# after that point -- not specific to this CVE, this binding, or
# jupyterhub: any large, docstring/config-heavy Python module (Django
# settings, Flask app factories, any Traitlets-configured class, which is
# exactly HubAuth's own shape) can silently hide code past the cutoff from
# every module-scope presence rule.
#
# Isolated CVE-independently, using the already-shipped VYQL-RF-044
# pipeline and no jupyterhub vocabulary at all: rank 1219's own IPython
# Notebook probe (AuthenticatedHandler.get_current_user calling
# get_secure_cookie, no Origin/xsrf check anywhere in the module), padded
# with unrelated PaddingHelper classes ahead of it, stops firing once
# enough padding classes precede it. 37 padding classes still fires; 38
# does not -- both below, as the test pair, confirming a general per-module
# token budget rather than anything about jupyterhub's or IPython's own
# class shape.
#
# Scanning the real checkout (bounded, timeout 180, --max-ram 4GB, --cache
# off, docs/jsx/share/testing/onbuild/dockerfiles/ci/examples/demo-image
# excluded so the scan finishes inside the bound) confirms the same absence
# at scale: no VYQL-RF-044 finding names auth.py at the vulnerable parent
# commit.
#
# Raising or removing the 512-token module-scope cap is two constants in
# one file, mirroring rank 1223's already-recorded class-scope entry, but
# not a bounded, risk-free change made inside one rank's review: an
# unbounded (or much larger) per-module token budget changes what every
# other already-shipped module-scope Python binding sees on every large
# real module in the corpus, and needs a precision/perf pass across the
# whole benchmark, not a bump here. Recorded rather than attempted; no
# definition change ships with this rank.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, isolating pair below.

test "rank1251: cookie-authenticated handler, with 37 unrelated padding classes ahead of it, is still caught"
  lang python
  file handlers.py
  expect VYQL-RF-044
  expect_review code.CookieAuthHandlerMissingPrepareOriginCheck
